---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import ProductCard from "../components/ProductCard.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Water Filtration Systems for Home",
    "url": "/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Under Sink Water Filters",
    "url": "/best-under-sink-water-filter/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Water Filter Pitchers",
    "url": "/best-water-filter-pitcher/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Gas Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-gas-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Heat Pump Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-heat-pump-water-heater/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-10", modified: "2026-11-10" });
const title = "Best Shower Head Filter in 2026: What They Remove, and What They Don't";
const description =
  "The best shower head filters for 2026, ranked on NSF/ANSI 177 chlorine certification, with an honest look at hard water, hot water and what KDF really does.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "sprite-ho2-wh",
    "name": "Sprite HO2-WH High Output Shower Filter",
    "category": "Inline filter with Chlorgon media, NSF/ANSI 177, 2.5 gpm",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Certification",
        "NSF/ANSI 177 (free available chlorine)"
      ],
      [
        "Media",
        "Chlorgon (non-carbon)"
      ],
      [
        "Rated Flow",
        "2.5 gpm"
      ],
      [
        "Cartridge Life",
        "6-12 months, per Sprite's manual"
      ],
      [
        "Replacement",
        "HOC cartridge (showerhead sold separately on HO2-WH)"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "Certified to NSF/ANSI 177 for chlorine reduction",
      "Media chosen for hot-water performance, according to Sprite's retailers",
      "Full 2.5 gpm flow, so pressure feels normal",
      "Keeps your existing showerhead",
      "Reversible cartridge and long-running design with cheap refills"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Plain white plastic body is not a design statement",
      "Adds several inches between the arm and the head",
      "Gallon ratings vary between retailers"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The certified, no-nonsense chlorine filter for anyone who wants to keep the showerhead they already like.",
    "description": "Sprite has been making shower filters for decades, and the High Output 2 is the one that keeps showing up in plumbing supply catalogs rather than influencer videos. It is an inline filter: it threads onto the shower arm and your existing head threads onto it. Sprite's retailers list it as certified to NSF/ANSI 177, the shower filter standard, which covers reduction of free available chlorine. It uses Sprite's Chlorgon media rather than carbon, and Sprite's retailers say that media was chosen because it holds up better on chlorine at shower temperatures. The rated flow is 2.5 gpm, the federal maximum for showerheads, so it does not choke a good head. Sprite's manual says to change the cartridge every six to 12 months depending on your water, and the cartridge is inexpensive. It will not soften water, and it will not stop scale, but it does the one job a shower filter can reliably do at a certified level. For most people annoyed by a chlorine smell or dry skin from city water, this is the first thing to try."
  },
  {
    "id": "jolie-filtered-showerhead",
    "name": "Jolie The Filtered Showerhead",
    "category": "All-in-one filtered showerhead, KDF-55 and calcium sulfite",
    "badge": "Best Filtered Showerhead",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Filter Media",
        "KDF-55 and calcium sulfite"
      ],
      [
        "Filter Life",
        "About 90 days, per Jolie"
      ],
      [
        "Flow Rate",
        "1.8 gpm (per listing)"
      ],
      [
        "Head Size",
        "5.3 in diameter"
      ],
      [
        "Certification",
        "Jolie says it exceeds NSF/ANSI 177; no listing found"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "Clean design that replaces your showerhead entirely",
      "Filter sits in the neck and swaps in seconds",
      "Calcium sulfite stage handles chlorine in hot water",
      "Owners commonly praise the even spray",
      "Subscription refills make it easy to stay on schedule"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Premium price for the head and the refills",
      "90-day filter life is short",
      "One independent test found zinc levels rose in the filtered water"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The filtered showerhead to buy if you want one good-looking unit and accept paying more for refills.",
    "description": "Jolie made the filtered showerhead a lifestyle product, and the hardware is better than the marketing suggests. It is a single unit with a stylish round head and a replaceable filter in the neck, so there is no inline canister. Jolie lists the media as KDF-55 and calcium sulfite, two media widely used in shower filters because they keep working on chlorine in hot water, and says the filter far exceeds NSF-177 requirements. That is a claim rather than a certification listing, so treat it accordingly. The filter is rated for about 90 days, which Jolie bases on a typical two-person household. Water Filter Guru's independent test is worth knowing about: it found strong chlorine reduction but also reported zinc in the filtered water rising sharply, which it attributed to the KDF media. Zinc at those levels is not considered a health concern for skin contact, but it shows that every media has side effects. Buy the Jolie for the design and the convenience. Buy the Sprite if you want a certified result for less."
  },
  {
    "id": "culligan-wsh-c125",
    "name": "Culligan WSH-C125 Wall-Mount Filtered Shower Head",
    "category": "Filtered showerhead with KDF media, NSF/ANSI 177",
    "badge": "Best Budget Certified",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Certification",
        "NSF/ANSI 177 (free available chlorine), per retailer listings"
      ],
      [
        "Media",
        "KDF"
      ],
      [
        "Cartridge Life",
        "10,000 gallons or 6 months"
      ],
      [
        "Rated Service Flow",
        "2.5 gpm, per Culligan's manual"
      ],
      [
        "Replacement",
        "WHR-140 cartridge"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "Certified chlorine reduction at a low starter price",
      "Long 10,000-gallon cartridge rating",
      "Head and filter in one unit, with massage spray settings",
      "Cartridge also fits Culligan's handheld HSH-C135"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Some retailers list it as discontinued, so stock varies",
      "Bulky, utilitarian look",
      "Sellers disagree on flow and warranty figures"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A certified, inexpensive all-in-one filtered head, as long as you can still find it in stock.",
    "description": "The Culligan WSH-C125 is the budget answer to the Jolie: a showerhead with a built-in KDF filter, at a fraction of the price, with certification paperwork behind it. Retailers list it as tested and certified by NSF International to NSF/ANSI 177 for reduction of free available chlorine, and rate the WHR-140 cartridge at 10,000 gallons or six months. Culligan's manual gives a rated service flow of 2.5 gpm, though some retailer listings show lower figures, and the head offers several spray patterns including a massage setting. The look is functional rather than stylish, and the plastic body is larger than designer heads. The main caution is availability: some retailers now list the model as discontinued, so check that replacement WHR-140 cartridges are in stock before you commit. Culligan markets reductions beyond chlorine, but the certification covers chlorine only, and that is what you should expect it to do. As a low-cost way to get a certified filtered shower, it is still hard to beat."
  },
  {
    "id": "kohler-k-22321",
    "name": "Kohler Aquifer K-22321 Shower Filter",
    "category": "Inline shower filter, NSF/ANSI 177, up to 4.0 gpm",
    "badge": "Best for High-Flow Heads",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Certification",
        "NSF/ANSI 177, per Kohler's replacement spec sheet"
      ],
      [
        "Max Flow",
        "4.0 gpm at 60 psi"
      ],
      [
        "Cartridge Life",
        "8,000 gallons or 6 months"
      ],
      [
        "Replacement",
        "K-22322 two-pack"
      ],
      [
        "Warranty",
        "1-year limited"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "Kohler build quality and matching finishes",
      "High flow rating will not bottleneck a good showerhead",
      "Works with almost any standard showerhead",
      "Clear gallon and time ratings on the spec sheet"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs more than the Sprite for the same basic job",
      "Six-month cartridge life",
      "Only a one-year warranty on the housing"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The inline filter to buy if you want it to match a Kohler or designer showerhead.",
    "description": "Kohler's Aquifer is the inline filter for people who care what the shower arm looks like. It sits between the arm and any standard showerhead and comes in finishes intended to match Kohler trim. Kohler's spec sheet for the replacement cartridges cites NSF/ANSI 177, and rates the media for up to 8,000 gallons or six months, with a maximum flow of 4.0 gpm at 60 psi, so it has plenty of headroom above the 2.5 gpm limit for showerheads and will not be the bottleneck. Kohler markets it for chlorine and odor reduction and scale control; treat the scale claim modestly, because a cartridge this size cannot soften water, and the certification covers chlorine. Replacement cartridges come as the K-22322 two-pack, which covers a year at the rated interval. The housing has a one-year limited warranty. Functionally it does the same job as the Sprite. The reason to pay more is finish and fit, especially if the rest of your bathroom is Kohler."
  },
  {
    "id": "canopy-filtered-showerhead",
    "name": "Canopy Filtered Showerhead",
    "category": "Filtered showerhead, carbon, KDF-55 and calcium sulfite",
    "badge": "Best for Easy Refills",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Filter Media",
        "Activated carbon, KDF-55, calcium sulfite"
      ],
      [
        "Filter Life",
        "Replace every 90 days"
      ],
      [
        "Certification",
        "Described as NSF-177 certified in listings"
      ],
      [
        "Refills",
        "Subscription or one-off"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "Three-media filter covers chlorine in both warm and hot water",
      "Clean design and simple filter swaps",
      "Refill subscription removes the need to remember",
      "Easy, tool-free install"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "An independent test found results below Canopy's claims",
      "No published flow rate in the sources we found",
      "Short 90-day filter life"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A well-designed, easy-to-live-with filtered head; buy it for convenience rather than for exceptional filtration.",
    "description": "Canopy, best known for its humidifiers, sells a filtered showerhead built on the same idea as the Jolie: one tidy unit, a cartridge in the neck, and a subscription to send refills. Canopy and its retailers describe the filter as an NSF-177 certified three-media design using activated carbon, KDF-55 and calcium sulfite, which is a sensible mix: carbon for organic compounds in cooler water, and KDF and calcium sulfite for chlorine at shower temperatures. Canopy says to replace the filter every 90 days. The independent evidence is less glowing than the marketing. Water Filter Guru's test reported results that did not line up with Canopy's claims, and pointed out that none of the media can address hardness. That matches our view of the category: expect chlorine reduction and a nicer-smelling shower, not transformed skin or softer water. Canopy is a good choice if the subscription and the design matter to you. If filtration performance per dollar is the priority, the Sprite and Culligan are better value."
  },
  {
    "id": "aquabliss-sf100",
    "name": "AquaBliss SF100 High Output Revitalizing Shower Filter",
    "category": "12-stage inline shower filter",
    "badge": "Most Popular Inline",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Stages",
        "12"
      ],
      [
        "Media",
        "Sediment filters, redox media, calcium sulfite, activated carbon, ceramic beads (per listing)"
      ],
      [
        "Capacity",
        "10,000 gallons (per listing)"
      ],
      [
        "Install",
        "Inline, fits most standard showerheads"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "Very low starting price",
      "Inline design keeps your current showerhead",
      "Calcium sulfite and redox media for chlorine",
      "Huge owner base and easy-to-find cartridges"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No active NSF/ANSI certification found",
      "12-stage marketing overstates what the media can do",
      "Filter life figures conflict between listings"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A cheap inline filter that will likely cut the chlorine smell, but without the certification the Sprite carries.",
    "description": "The AquaBliss SF100 is one of the best-selling shower filters on Amazon, and it is easy to see why: it is inexpensive, it threads on between the arm and your existing head, and the box promises 12 stages of filtration. Listings name the media as sediment filters, redox media, calcium sulfite, activated carbon and mineral ceramic beads, and give a capacity of 10,000 gallons. The redox media and calcium sulfite are both credible chlorine reducers in warm water, so owners commonly report a less chlorinated smell after installing it. Where the SF100 falls short is evidence. We found no active NSF/ANSI 177 certification for it, and a third-party certification database lists none. The 12-stage count is marketing: several of those stages are screens and beads that do little to the chemistry of the water. Filter life is also listed inconsistently across sellers. If you want the cheapest reasonable inline filter, this is it. If you want a certified result at a similar price, choose the Sprite HO2."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Shower Head Filter",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-shower-head-filter/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do shower head filters really work?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For chlorine, yes. Filters certified to NSF/ANSI 177, such as the Sprite HO2-WH and Culligan WSH-C125, are tested for free chlorine reduction, and owners commonly report less chlorine smell. They do not soften hard water, and most do little for chloramine. Claims about lead or heavy metals are not covered by the shower filter standard."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best shower head filter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, the Sprite HO2-WH: it is certified to NSF/ANSI 177, flows at 2.5 gpm, keeps your existing showerhead and has inexpensive cartridges. If you want an all-in-one filtered shower head instead, the Jolie is the best designed, and the Culligan WSH-C125 is the budget certified option."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do shower filters remove hard water?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Hardness is dissolved calcium and magnesium, which takes ion exchange in a water softener to remove. A small shower cartridge cannot remove a meaningful amount at shower flow rates. Some filters claim to reduce scale, but that is not certified and should not be expected. For hard water, a whole-house softener is the fix."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do shower filters remove chloramine?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most do it poorly. Chloramine is more stable than chlorine and reacts slowly with KDF and carbon in the brief contact time of a shower. Vitamin C filters are the main exception, because ascorbic acid neutralises chloramine quickly, but their cartridges are used up fast. Check your utility's water report to see which disinfectant it uses."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should you change a shower filter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "At the rated interval, which ranges from about 90 days for the Jolie and Canopy to six months for the Culligan and Kohler and six to 12 months for the Sprite. Long showers, more people and very hot water shorten that. Media like calcium sulfite and vitamin C stop working once they are used up, even though the water keeps flowing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does a shower filter reduce water pressure?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A well-sized one should not. Inline filters rated at or above your showerhead's flow, like the Sprite at 2.5 gpm or the Kohler at 4.0 gpm, are not the restriction. Filtered heads list their own flow, and a 1.8 gpm head will feel gentler than an old 2.5 gpm head. A sudden pressure drop usually means a clogged cartridge or scaled nozzles."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are filtered shower heads good for hair and skin?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They may help people who are sensitive to chlorine, and many owners report softer hair and less dry skin. But there is little controlled research, and hard water, hot showers and soap affect skin and hair as much as chlorine does. Buy a shower filter to reduce chlorine, and treat any cosmetic improvement as a bonus rather than a promise."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Water, Plumbing & Electrical",
    tags: [
      "best shower head filter",
      "shower filter",
      "filtered shower head",
      "shower water filter",
      "chlorine shower filter",
      "nsf 177 shower filter"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          Best Shower Head Filter
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          Six shower filters ranked on what the evidence supports: chlorine reduction, certified under NSF/ANSI 177 where the maker has paid for it, and no promises about softening hard water.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          We judge every product on its spec sheet, its label and what owners report over time, not on the marketing. No paid placements, and commission never sets a ranking. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">11 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>The Short Answer</span></a>
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>The 6 Best Shower Head Filters</span></a>
              <a href="#what-they-remove" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>What Shower Filters Can Remove</span></a>
              <a href="#what-they-dont" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>What They Cannot Do</span></a>
              <a href="#comparison" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>Side-by-Side Comparison</span></a>
              <a href="#buying-guide" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Buying Guide: Specs That Matter</span></a>
              <a href="#inline-vs-head" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Inline Filter or Filtered Head?</span></a>
              <a href="#install-and-care" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Installation and Cartridge Care</span></a>
              <a href="#bigger-fix" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>When You Need a Bigger Fix</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A shower head filter does one job well: it reduces chlorine. It does not soften hard water, it does not make well water safe, and most of the long contaminant lists on shower filter boxes are not backed by any certification. Buy the Sprite HO2 if you want a certified, inexpensive inline filter that keeps your current head, and the Jolie if you want an all-in-one filtered showerhead.
