        <div id="what-they-remove" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Shower Filters Can Remove
          </h2>

          <p>
            The only certification written specifically for shower filters is NSF/ANSI 177, and it covers one thing: reduction of free available chlorine. That tells you where the evidence is. Chlorine is what shower filters are designed for, and the media inside them is chosen to deal with it in water that is hot, fast-moving and in contact with the filter for a fraction of a second.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">KDF-55</h3>

          <p>
            KDF-55 is a copper-zinc alloy in granular form. It reduces chlorine through a redox reaction, converting it to chloride, and its makers and the filter brands that use it say it keeps working at shower temperatures. It also tends to slow bacterial growth inside the cartridge. Its side effect is that small amounts of zinc can end up in the water, which Water Filter Guru measured in its test of the Jolie. At the levels involved this is not considered a health issue for showering, but it is a reminder that every media trades one thing for another.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Calcium sulfite</h3>

          <p>
            Calcium sulfite reacts quickly with chlorine and, according to the brands that use it, performs well in hot water. That is why it appears in the Jolie, Canopy and AquaBliss filters. It is consumed as it works, which is one reason these cartridges need changing every few months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vitamin C</h3>

          <p>
            Ascorbic acid, vitamin C, neutralises both chlorine and chloramine very quickly, and it is a well-established dechlorination method; some water utilities and fire departments use it to dechlorinate water before discharge. Its weakness is that it dissolves and is used up fast, so vitamin C cartridges are small and short-lived. If your water is chloraminated, a vitamin C filter is the most likely type to make a noticeable difference, but check the cartridge life and cost before committing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Activated carbon</h3>

          <p>
            Carbon is the workhorse of drinking water filters, but it is a weaker performer in a shower. Water filter makers, including the brands that choose other media for their shower products, note that carbon adsorbs chlorine less effectively as water gets hotter, and the contact time in a shower is very short. Carbon in a shower filter is a supporting stage, not the main event.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-they-dont" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Shower Filters Cannot Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the section most shower filter listings leave out, and it is the reason so many buyers are disappointed after a month.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>They do not soften hard water.</strong> Hardness is dissolved calcium and magnesium. Removing it requires ion exchange, which is what a salt-based water softener does with a tank of resin the size of a small refrigerator, regenerated with brine. A shower cartridge a few inches long, passing water at 2.5 gallons a minute, cannot remove a meaningful amount of hardness for more than a few minutes. Some filters claim to reduce scale through media such as KDF; at best that changes how scale forms rather than removing the minerals. If your shower glass is spotted and your showerhead nozzles clog with white crust, a shower filter will not change that.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>They do not make unsafe water safe.</strong> Shower filters are not designed or certified to remove bacteria, viruses or protozoa. If you are on a well that tests positive for bacteria, the fix is well treatment, not a filter on the shower arm.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Most do not handle chloramine well.</strong> Chloramine is more stable than chlorine and reacts slowly with KDF and carbon in the short contact time a shower allows. Vitamin C is the main exception. If your utility uses chloramine, expect a smaller difference than the reviews from chlorinated areas suggest.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The long contaminant lists are mostly unverified.</strong> Many listings claim reductions in lead, heavy metals, fluoride, VOCs and microplastics. NSF/ANSI 177 does not test any of those, and shower filters are not certified to drinking water standards. A shower cartridge with a contact time measured in fractions of a second is not a lead filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The skin and hair claims are anecdotal.</strong> Many owners report softer hair and less irritated skin, and reducing chlorine plausibly helps people who are sensitive to it. But there is little controlled research, and results vary with your water and your skin. Buy a shower filter to reduce chlorine, and treat any cosmetic improvement as a bonus.
          </p>
        </div>

