        <div id="buying-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Buying Guide: The Specs That Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Five things separate a useful shower water filter from an expensive placebo. Work through them in this order.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1. Certification to NSF/ANSI 177</h3>

          <p>
            It is the only certification that applies to shower filters, so it is the closest thing to proof that a filter does what it says. Look for the certifier's name and the model number on the listing, and be cautious of wording such as 'meets' or 'exceeds' the standard, which usually means the maker ran its own test rather than holding a listing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2. Media matched to your water</h3>

          <p>
            For chlorinated water, KDF-55, calcium sulfite or a non-carbon media such as Sprite's Chlorgon all work. For chloraminated water, look for vitamin C and accept shorter cartridge life. A filter that leans mainly on activated carbon is the weakest choice for hot showers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">3. Cartridge life and refill cost</h3>

          <p>
            Check the rated gallons and months, and work out the annual refill cost. A household of two showering daily can use well over 10,000 gallons a year in the shower, so a 10,000-gallon cartridge may not last its full time rating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">4. Flow rate</h3>

          <p>
            Inline filters should be rated at or above your showerhead's flow so they are not the restriction. Filtered heads list their own flow; 1.8 gpm is gentle, 2.5 gpm is the federal maximum.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">5. Inline or filtered head</h3>

          <p>
            If you like your showerhead, or it is a rain head or a handheld, an inline filter keeps it. If your showerhead is old and you want a single neat unit, a filtered head replaces it. The next section covers the trade-offs in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inline-vs-head" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Inline Filter or Filtered Shower Head?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are two physical designs, and they suit different bathrooms. An inline filter is a canister that threads onto the shower arm, with your existing head threaded onto it. A filtered shower head has the cartridge built into the head or its neck, and replaces your current head entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inline filters, such as the Sprite HO2, Kohler Aquifer and AquaBliss SF100, have three advantages. They work with almost any showerhead, including rain heads and handhelds; they usually hold more media, which means longer cartridge life; and they tend to be cheaper to run. The drawbacks are looks and length. An inline canister adds several inches to the arm, which can push a head too low for tall people or too close to a sliding door. Check the clearance before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Filtered heads, such as the Jolie, Canopy and Culligan WSH-C125, are tidier and easier to install, because you only swap one part. The trade-off is a smaller cartridge, which is why the premium heads quote filter lives around 90 days, and you lose whatever head you had before. If you have a high-end head you love, keep it and go inline.
          </p>

          <p>
            For handheld showers, an inline filter can go between the arm and the hose diverter, or between the hose and the handheld, depending on the fittings. Culligan's WHR-140 cartridge also fits its handheld HSH-C135, which is a simple route if you want a filtered handheld. For a rain head mounted on a long arm, inline is almost always the only option.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever design you choose, the media inside matters more than the shape outside. A certified inline filter on a basic head will reduce more chlorine than a beautiful head with an uncertified cartridge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install-and-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Installation and Cartridge Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every filter on this list installs without tools beyond a wrench, and most without any tools at all. Unscrew the old showerhead, clean the threads on the arm, wrap them clockwise with two or three turns of plumber's tape, and thread the filter or new head on hand-tight. For an inline filter, tape its outlet threads and thread your head onto it. Run the water for a minute or two as the manual directs to flush the new cartridge.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you use a wrench to tighten, wrap the jaws in a cloth so you do not scar a plated finish, and do not overtighten plastic housings; a quarter turn past hand-tight is usually enough. A drip at the joint almost always means not enough tape or cross-threading rather than a faulty filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cartridge care is mostly about timing. Media such as calcium sulfite and vitamin C are consumed as they work, so an exhausted cartridge does nothing at all. Change cartridges at the rated interval, and sooner if your household takes long showers. A reminder on your phone the day you install a cartridge is the easiest way to keep track.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hot water heaters are worth a thought here. Most shower filter media are rated for normal shower temperatures, but very hot water shortens cartridge life and pushes carbon media past its useful range. If your water heater is set well above about 120°F, turning it down is a safety improvement anyway and helps the filter. If you are replacing the heater, our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">gas water heater guide</a> covers sizing and temperature settings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, a filter will not stop a showerhead clogging with scale. If the nozzles are crusted, soak the head in white vinegar for an hour and brush the nozzles. On hard water, that is a maintenance job every few months whether you filter or not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bigger-fix" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When You Need a Bigger Fix
          </h2>

          <p>
            A shower filter is a small, cheap fix for one problem. Some problems need something larger, and spending on a shower filter first only delays the real fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hard water everywhere.</strong> Scale on the shower glass, white crust on taps, spotted dishes and a kettle that needs descaling all point to hardness. A salt-based softener on the main supply is the only reliable fix. It treats every tap and appliance at once, and it is covered alongside other point-of-entry systems in our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">home water filtration guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Smell or staining at every tap.</strong> A rotten-egg smell, orange staining or sediment in every fixture points to iron, manganese or hydrogen sulfide, common on wells. Those need whole-house treatment, often an oxidising filter, and a shower filter will be overwhelmed within weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Chlorine in drinking water too.</strong> If you dislike the chlorine taste at the kitchen tap as much as the smell in the shower, a whole-house carbon filter treats both at once and can be more economical than separate devices. If drinking water is the bigger worry, start with a certified kitchen filter; our <a href="/best-under-sink-water-filter/">under sink water filter guide</a> ranks them by certification, and our <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">water filter pitcher guide</a> covers renters.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skin conditions that do not improve.</strong> If dry skin or eczema persists after a month with a working filter, the cause may be hard water, soap, hot showers or something unrelated to the water. A dermatologist is a better next step than a more expensive filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, with chlorinated city water and a shower that smells like a swimming pool, a certified shower filter is one of the cheapest, simplest upgrades in the house. Buy one certified to NSF/ANSI 177, change the cartridge on time, and judge it on chlorine.
          </p>
        </div>

