            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you change a shower filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At the rated interval, which ranges from about 90 days for the Jolie and Canopy to six months for the Culligan and Kohler and six to 12 months for the Sprite. Long showers, more people and very hot water shorten that. Media like calcium sulfite and vitamin C stop working once they are used up, even though the water keeps flowing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a shower filter reduce water pressure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A well-sized one should not. Inline filters rated at or above your showerhead's flow, like the Sprite at 2.5 gpm or the Kohler at 4.0 gpm, are not the restriction. Filtered heads list their own flow, and a 1.8 gpm head will feel gentler than an old 2.5 gpm head. A sudden pressure drop usually means a clogged cartridge or scaled nozzles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are filtered shower heads good for hair and skin?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They may help people who are sensitive to chlorine, and many owners report softer hair and less dry skin. But there is little controlled research, and hard water, hot showers and soap affect skin and hair as much as chlorine does. Buy a shower filter to reduce chlorine, and treat any cosmetic improvement as a bonus rather than a promise.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
